            'excerpt' => 'Buying a CO2 laser cutter for acrylic, plywood or batch work? See which specs really matter, how the xTool P3 and X1 differ and what your workshop needs.',
            'blocks'  => [
                [ 'p', 'Choose a CO₂ laser cutter by your materials, sheet size and production volume. For acrylic, plywood, leather and paper, what matters most is power, bed size, automatic material positioning and proper fume extraction – not the top speed in the brochure. And a CO₂ laser of this class won’t cut metal.' ],
                [ 'p', 'This guide is for readers who have already chosen CO₂ over a diode laser – we compare laser types in a separate article. We offer two such xTool machines: the P3, an 80 W flagship CO₂ laser cutter, and the X1, a platform with swappable laser sources. Here is what to look for in the specs, how the two differ and what to prepare in your workshop.' ],
                [ 'h2', 'What does a CO₂ laser cut, and what does it only engrave?' ],
                [ 'p', 'A CO₂ laser works in the infrared at a wavelength of 10.6 µm, which organic materials, plastics and glass absorb well. If you need a laser for cutting acrylic, CO₂ is the right choice.' ],
                [ 'ul', [
                    'Acrylic (PMMA, Perspex), including clear: the laser cuts it with a smooth, glossy edge that needs no polishing. Cast acrylic engraves to a frosted white; cheaper extruded acrylic cuts well, but engravings on it show less contrast.',
                    'Wood, plywood and MDF: cutting and engraving. Edges darken, and the glue in plywood affects how evenly it cuts, so use laser-grade plywood for production.',
